    {
      "actor": "assessment",
      "kind": "decision",
      "summary": "Baseline ran first: I unzipped the source (fil_jqh5dcq65z6ch7e3wy59v6t312), read repo/planner.py (Dijkstra over an undirected weighted graph via heapq; queue entries are tuples (distance, route, node) so ties break by lexicographically-smaller route, then node; unknown start/end returns {\"error\":\"unknown node\"}; negative/non-numeric edge weight returns {\"error\":\"invalid edge\"}; no path returns {\"distance\":null,\"route\":[]}; output is json.dumps with ensure_ascii=False preserving Unicode), and executed every preserve input against python3 planner.py to capture real outputs. All suggested preserve outputs matched the expected baseline: weighted 5/[\"A\",\"B\",\"D\"], reverse 5/[\"D\",\"B\",\"A\"], same-node 0/[\"A\"], unknown-node {\"error\":\"unknown node\"}, disconnected null/[], invalid-weight {\"error\":\"invalid edge\"}, unicode 2.5/[\"café\",\"倉庫\"]. Both blocked-edge requirement expectations were validated against the source's routing semantics: blocking B-D reroutes to A->B->C->D at distance 7, and blocking both edges out of A yields unreachable null/[].\n\nAdditions beyond the suggested contract, justified by explicit customer requirements: (1) a tie-break preserve check, since the objective calls out preserving ties and no suggested check exercises equal-cost routes — I ran an equal-cost A-B-D/A-C-D graph against the Python source and it returned 2/[\"A\",\"B\",\"D\"], confirming tie-break is by lexicographically-smaller route, which the Node port must reproduce. (2) a fractional-distance preserve check, since the objective explicitly names fractional distances and the unicode case is the only suggested one using them. (3) a blocked-reverse-pair requirement check, since blocked_edges are undirected — the README field description and the code's symmetry require that [\"D\",\"B\"] blocks the same link as [\"B\",\"D\"]; without this check a forward-only comparison would silently pass.\n\ntarget_build is empty: planner.mjs uses only Node.js built-in modules and needs no build or install step; node v24.22.1 is installed. source_build is empty: planner.py runs directly under python3 3.12.3. No unmet requirements — every requested behavior (shortest route, validation, ties, unreachable, Unicode, fractional distances, blocked_edges reroute/unreachable/reverse-pair) is exercised by a check.",
