    {
      "actor": "implementation",
      "kind": "decision",
      "summary": "Migrated planner.py to planner.mjs, a Node.js CLI using only the built-in node:fs module (no external dependencies). The port preserves the original Dijkstra shortest-route behavior byte-for-byte, including: unknown-node error (checked before edge validation), invalid-edge error (unknown endpoint or non-numeric/negative weight; booleans accepted as numbers like Python's isinstance), lexicographic tie-break via a heapq-equivalent binary heap with tuple comparison (distance, route, node), unreachable routes as {\"distance\": null, \"route\": []}, Unicode node names (raw UTF-8 output, ensure_ascii=false equivalent), and fractional distances — including emitting \"5.0\" for a fractional sum that is whole, which required a small built-in JSON parser that tags numeric literals as int vs float since JSON.parse loses that distinction. Malformed (non-JSON) stdin yields {\"error\":\"invalid request\"}; valid-JSON non-object input (null/array) aborts with no stdout, matching the Python source's uncaught AttributeError.\n\nNew feature: optional blocked_edges field, a list of undirected [a,b] pairs that must never be traversed, endpoint-order independent ([\"A\",\"C\"] and [\"C\",\"A\"] block the same edge). Blocked edges force rerouting; if every path to the destination is blocked, the destination is reported unreachable.\n\nOriginal planner.py and README.md are preserved in original/ for reference. A new README.md documents execution and the blocked_edges field.\n\nVerification: a 28-case differential test comparing planner.py vs planner.mjs output byte-for-byte passed 28/28. A 10-case blocked-edge test passed 10/10. A 9-case cross-check against a Python reference extended with the same blocked_edges semantics passed 9/9. All 12 public command-interface scenarios (9 preserve + 3 requirement) produce correct output. node --check confirms planner.mjs syntax.",
      "origin": "agent",
      "at": "2026-11-07T23:41:38.571Z"
